      "math_academy_id": "q-77891",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2]?$",
      "worked_solution": "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[-2,2],$ the function must be continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2).$\n\nFirst, notice that the function $s(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ and differentiable on $(-2,2)$, so it satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nLet's check each of the other functions individually.\n\n- The function $f(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has a corner point at $x=1.$ Hence, $f(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $f(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)$ is continuous on $[-2,2]$ but has corner points at $x=\\pm 1.$ Hence, $h(x)$ is not differentiable at $x=\\pm 1,$ which means it is not differentiable on $(-2,2).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $p(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Hence, $p(x)$ is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $p(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $t(x)$ has a vertical asymptote at $x=1.$ Hence, $t(x)$  is not continuous on $[-2,2].$ Therefore, $t(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Correct",
